          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What kind of physical activity is described for people with type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "The guidelines on physical activity for individuals with type 2 diabetes recommend incorporating regular aerobic exercise into daily life. Here are some key points from the findings:\n\n1. **Regular Aerobic Activity**: It is recommended to engage in regular aerobic activities most days of the week. This can help improve overall health and manage blood sugar levels.\n\n2. **Strength Training**: Incorporating muscle-strengthening activities on two or more days a week is also beneficial. This can help maintain muscle mass and promote better blood sugar control.\n\n3. **Supervised Programs**: Structured exercise programs, preferably with some level of supervision, may offer better adherence and outcomes for those with type 2 diabetes.\n\n4. **Individualized Approach**: Recommendations should be tailored to each individual's abilities and preferences. The goal is to make physical activity a part of daily life.\n\nThese points are based on a synthesis of guidance from various diabetes and health authorities, focusing on the importance of both aerobic and strength-training exercises. Always discuss with a healthcare provider to create a personalized exercise plan that aligns with individual health circumstances. \n\nThese insights can serve as a foundational understanding, but it is vital for individuals to consult with healthcare professionals for tailored advice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
